11
00:57:23,478 --> 00:57:29,478
BlackBerry is a 2023 Canadian biographical comedy-drama film directed by Matt
Johnson from a screenplay by Johnson and producer Matthew Miller.

12
01:02:36,521 --> 01:02:42,521
It was loosely adapted from Jacquie McNish and Sean Silcoff's book Losing the Signal: The
Untold Story Behind the Extraordinary Rise and Spectacular Fall of BlackBerry.

13
01:07:49,565 --> 01:07:55,565
The film is a dramatized account of the history of the BlackBerry line of mobile phones created
by co-founders Douglas Fregin and Mike Lazaridis, and investor Jim Balsillie.

14
01:13:02,608 --> 01:13:08,608
Lazaridis is portrayed by Jay Baruchel, Balsillie is portrayed by
Glenn Howerton, and Fregin is portrayed by Johnson.

15
01:18:15,652 --> 01:18:21,652
The film also stars Rich Sommer, Michael Ironside, Martin Donovan, Michelle Giroux,
SungWon Cho, Mark Critch, Saul Rubinek, and Cary Elwes in supporting roles.

16
01:23:28,695 --> 01:23:34,695
BlackBerry premiered in competition at the 73rd Berlin
International Film Festival on February 17, 2023.

17
01:28:41,739 --> 01:28:47,739
The film was released in Canada on May 12, 2023, to positive reviews.

18
01:33:54,782 --> 01:34:00,782
In late 2023, Blackberry was re-released as a
three-part miniseries with additional footage.

19
01:39:07,826 --> 01:39:13,826
The film is the most nominated film in the history of the
Canadian Screen Awards, with 17 nominations.

20
01:44:20,869 --> 01:44:26,869
It won 14 awards, including Best Motion Picture.

21
01:49:33,913 --> 01:49:39,913
The film explores themes of based on novel or book,
technology, it-expert, silicon valley, satire.

22
01:54:46,956 --> 01:54:52,956
The tone is lighthearted and emotional.
